### Code-switching, whole phrases

{% sample title="Clip 2a · Code-switching, whole phrases" %}
**Text:** Lucía said, and I quote, "el martes es imposible." Klaus just replied "na ja, Donnerstag geht auch." Et la réservation ? C'est déjà fait.

**Listen for:** Accent and prosody at each switch into Spanish, German, and French; the name Lucía.

**Roughly how it sounds:** Lucía is loo-SEE-ah. Spanish “el martes es imposible” (“Tuesday is impossible”): el MAR-tess ess eem-po-SEE-bleh. German “na ja, Donnerstag geht auch” (“oh well, Thursday works too”): NAH-yah, a shrug, often run together as one word; then DON-ers-tahk gate OWKH, the final ch a breathy sound as in Scottish “loch”. French “Et la réservation ? C'est déjà fait.” (“And the reservation? It's already done.”): ay lah ray-zair-vah-SYOHN? say day-ZHAH FEH.

**Direction:** none; every model read it plain.
{% /sample %}

- {% model name="gpt-4o-mini-tts (2025-03-20)" vendor="openai" /%} **Plain.**

  {% audio src="/assets/tts-comparison/gpt-4o-mini-tts-2025-03-20__2a-languages__plain__0.webm" /%}

